 "all_passed": true,
 "checked_text": "we're answering: where do 1.5 a and 7.5 v come from? i'll circle the single series loop, because the same current flows through both resistors.\n\nin series, resistances add. so the battery pushes through r = 3 ohms plus r = 5 ohms, making 8 ohms total.\n3+5=8 ω\n\nnow use ohm's law on the whole loop: current equals voltage divided by total resistance. so 12 volts divided by 8 ohms gives 1.5 amps.\ni=12/8=1.5 a\ndivide by 8ω\n\nfor the voltage across r, use only the 5 ohm resistor. multiply the shared current, 1.5 a, by r = 5 ohms, and you get 7.5 volts.\n1.5×5=7.5 v\nv=ir\n\nthe internal resistor also uses up voltage: 1.5 a times 3 ohms equals 4.5 v. the two drops, 4.5 v and 7.5 v, add back to the 12 v source.\n4.5+7.5=12\n\nso the current is 1.5 a because the total resistance is 8 ohms. then the voltage across r is 7.5 v because that same current flows through the 5 ohm resistor.\n12 v source -> 3ω + 5ω\n3ω + 5ω -> 8ω total\n8ω total -> i = 1.5 a\ni = 1.5 a -> across 5ω\nacross 5ω -> 7.5 v"
}